            <ArticleTitle>The association of pain with aerobic fitness in patients with chronic low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1467-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the association of aerobic fitness (VO(2)max) with pain intensity as reported by a sample of patients with chronic low back pain (LBP).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional with partial longitudinal follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient interdisciplinary pain management program in a teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A convenience sample of 75 patients with chronic LBP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients reported pain intensity before and after undergoing a modified treadmill test. Peak VO(2) was measured by using indirect calorimetry. Predicted VO(2)max was determined by extrapolating peak VO(2) and heart rate values during testing to predicted maximal heart rate.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Aerobic fitness and pain intensity before and after testing.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No significant relation was found between pain intensity and predicted VO(2)max or aerobic fitness.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There is no association between pain intensity and aerobic fitness. Deconditioning, defined as a lack of cardiovascular fitness levels normal for age and gender, therefore does not contribute to pain intensity in patients with chronic LBP.</AbstractText>
